{
  "title": "What a Determinant Measures",
  "dek": "Drag the columns of a 2×2 matrix and watch the unit square's area become the determinant.",
  "tags": [
    "Linear Algebra",
    "Interactive"
  ],
  "cover": "/blog/what-a-determinant-measures/cover.webp",
  "coverAlt": "Close-up of the Determinant Explorer on Flip: the unit square turned into a mirrored parallelogram on a sheared grid.",
  "status": "draft",
  "createdAt": "2026-10-04T08:00:00.000Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-10-04T08:00:00.000Z",
  "publishedAt": null,
  "doc": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A 2×2 matrix sends the plane to itself. Its two columns, "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "u",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "v",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", say where the basis vectors land, and every other point follows along. The determinant is one number that summarizes what happened to area along the way."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Area Is the Whole Story"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The unit square spanned by the original basis vectors has area 1. After the matrix acts, that square becomes the parallelogram spanned by "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "u",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "v",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". Its area is "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "|det A|",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", and because the map is linear, every other region scales by the same factor."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "embed",
        "attrs": {
          "kind": "determinant",
          "props": {
            "preset": "shear"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Try the presets. A shear leans the square without changing its area, so the determinant stays at 1 even though the matrix is far from the identity. A stretch scales area by exactly the stretch factor."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The Sign Is Orientation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use Flip and push the slider past zero. The parallelogram passes through a sliver and comes out mirrored: the arc from "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "u",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " to "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "v",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " now turns clockwise. A negative determinant means the map reverses orientation."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Zero Means Collapse"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use Collapse and drag the slider to the end. When "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "v",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " lands on the line through "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "u",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "code"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", the plane flattens onto a line. No column is zero, yet the determinant is 0 and the matrix has no inverse."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The determinant is the factor by which a linear map scales area, with a sign for orientation."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
